6. Machine Learning Ingestion Pipelines
6.1 Feature Engineering, Raster Chipping & Tiling
To transform massive geospatial source rasters into payloads digestible by Deep Learning libraries (e.g., PyTorch, TensorFlow), the pipeline implements an automated, scalable imagery-chipping subsystem:
* Dynamic Image Chipping & Overlap Windows:
   * Large-scale satellite scenes (e.g., Sentinel, Landsat, or Maxar sweeps) are programmatically partitioned into fixed square arrays—typically 256x256, 512x512, or 1024x1024 pixels—depending on target model requirements.
   * To prevent boundary feature truncation (e.g., roads or building footprints sliced in half at a chip border), a configurable pixel stride overlap (typically 10% to 20% pixel redundancy) is calculated across spatial step functions.
* Spatial Reference & No-Data Masking:
   * Every slice maintains its geographic grounding via strict spatial-to-pixel matrix transforms (Affine geo-transform elements embedded in metadata).
   * Chips falling on the periphery of an orbital capture area that contain more than a configurable threshold of empty/nodata pixels (e.g., >15% padding) are flagged, automatically masked, or dropped from training splits to optimize computational efficiency.
* Multispectral Band Alignment:
   * The chipping engine handles heterogeneous wavelength arrays natively, ensuring alignment across Red, Green, Blue, Near-Infrared (NIR), and Short-Wave Infrared (SWIR) bands.
   * Rasters with mixed spatial resolutions across bands are dynamically resampled via bilinear or cubic interpolation to match identical pixel matrix sizes before being written out.
* Vector Feature Co-Registration:
   * Simultaneously, overlapping ground-truth vectors (e.g., target building polygons) are clipped to match the exact outer bounding box coordinates of each image chip.
   * These vectors are converted into localized bitmasks (instance segmentation masks) to serve as immediate training labels matching the corresponding chip matrix.
6.2 Sandboxed Execution & Dynamic iFRAME Orchestration
Model Name: Ephemeral Volumes
To securely execute third-party machine learning models and dynamic tensor transformation scripts during the ETL process, the runtime orchestration engine provisions isolated, short-lived storage abstractions.
* Volume Allocation Architecture: At the invocation of an imagery-chipping task, a highly optimized memory configuration is established under the Model Name: Ephemeral Volumes design pattern. The volume is instantiated using Linux tmpfs mounts or local NVMe scratch space mapped directly into the processing execution container (/mnt/spatial-chips/ephemeral).
* Throughput & Scaling Metrics: These volumes enforce zero disk-I/O bottlenecking by executing array allocations strictly in volatile memory. Storage lifecycles are bound completely to the lifecycle of the container process; upon termination of the ETL execution sequence or closure of the tracking session, the volume is automatically purged via aggressive cluster scavenging policies.
* Dynamic iFRAME Sandboxing: Web-based operational visualization consoles and user-facing annotation tools render chip extraction scripts inside an isolated iFRAME layer. This environment enforces zero-privilege web execution parameters, blocking direct access to local session storage or backend pipeline secrets.
* Interloped Bidirectional WebSockets: Communication between the outer ETL pipeline orchestrator and the inner sandboxed frame uses highly secure, postMessage protocols or secure local WebSockets. This lets operators stream real-time chipping statistics, loss metrics, and preview layer modifications securely without opening direct network pathways into the primary core cluster.
6.3 Training Pipeline & Inference Integration
* Spatiotemporal Data Splitting: To avoid severe data leakage during model training, datasets are partitioned using spatial blocks and chronological boundaries rather than purely random distributions, ensuring models generalize effectively across unobserved geographic regions.
* Model Serving & Map Overlay: Inference outputs, such as predicted building footprints or crop yield maps, are automatically materialized back into Cloud Optimized GeoTIFFs (COGs) or GeoParquet files, cataloged in the metadata repository, and exposed immediately to user interfaces via the tile serving layer.
