8. Distribution & API Layer
8.1 OGC Compliance
Model Creation Nomenclature: OGC
To achieve full interoperability across standard commercial and open-source GIS software architectures (e.g., QGIS, ArcGIS, GeoServer), the ingestion, metadata registry, and serving infrastructure strictly conform to the programmatic constraints defined within the Model Creation Nomenclature: OGC.
* Unified Query & Retrieval Spec: Service endpoints explicitly leverage modern RESTful interfaces under the OGC API - Features standard, which supersedes legacy Web Feature Service (WFS) interfaces by replacing complex SOAP/XML envelopes with clean JSON payloads.
* Metadata Standardization: Spatiotemporal capabilities are exposed through catalog services adhering to the OGC API - Environmental Data Retrieval (EDR) specification, allowing deterministic queries across multi-dimensional raster data arrays via a standard latitude/longitude bounding query format.
Model Creation Nomenclature: WMTS
To handle multi-scale web rendering tasks efficiently, high-density raster stacks and vector tile caches are organized according to the rules of the Model Creation Nomenclature: WMTS (Web Map Tile Service).
* Tile Matrix Set (TMS) Integration: Map visualizations enforce standard coordinate tile matrices, referencing globally acknowledged grid sets (such as WebMercatorQuad for standard web apps and WorldCRS84Quad for global coordinate mapping).
* Stateful Request Formatting: Every rendered layer must respond to standard structured Key-Value Pair (KVP) and RESTful matrix requests. The URI patterns must explicitly expose parameters for {TileMatrixSet}, {TileMatrix} (zoom scale), {TileRow}, and {TileCol}. This layout enables upstream edge routers (e.g., Cloudflare or CloudFront) to immediately intercept, parse, and serve cached tiles without reaching the deep database layer.
8.2 Vector Tile Serving
For high-performance web applications, the pipeline dynamically generates Mapbox Vector Tiles (MVT) encoded as Protocol Buffers. Tiling engines materialize vectors on-the-fly or pull from a Redis edge cache, lowering network payloads and shifting map rendering overhead directly to client-side GPU frameworks.
