9. Security, Scalability & Monitoring
9.1 Attribute-Based Access Control
Model Creation Nomenclature: ABAC
To replace rigid, static Role-Based Access Control structures that fail when confronted with complex global jurisdictional constraints, the pipeline enforces real-time spatial policy evaluation under the Model Creation Nomenclature: ABAC.
* Multi-Dimensional Policy Rule Sets: Access tokens evaluate a dynamic tuple consisting of Subject Attributes, Object Attributes, and Environment Attributes:
   * Subject Attributes: User Clearance Level, Organizational Division, Subscribed Regional Bounds.
   * Object Attributes: Dataset H3 Resolution Layer, Geofence Boundaries, Data Classification Level (Public, Internal, Classified).
   * Environment Attributes: Current Unix Timestamp, Ingress Network IP, Request Mechanism (API Gateway vs. Interactive Console).
* Row-Level & Cell-Level Filtering Mechanics: When a user or automated microservice executes a query, the policy engine injects security predicates into the database runtime execution tree. For example, if an operator's profile restricts visibility to a specific metropolitan boundary, the system dynamically Appends an ST_Contains() check or an array of permitted H3 index markers directly to the query's SQL or GeoParquet scan path, filtering out unapproved entities before memory compilation.
9.2 Infrastructure Scalability & Auditing
* Auto-Scaling Compute: Containerized processing worker instances (deployed via Kubernetes) scale horizontally based on the size of the ingestion queue, maintaining pipeline velocity during sudden peaks in data delivery.
* Data Lineage Auditing: Every mutation of a geospatial entity records provenance metadata, tracking the raw source file, processing timestamp, and the specific version of the spatial ETL code utilized.